A||||10||Urchin||Moral dilema.....||||1||
Z||000000||Urchin||07-29-2001||09:00 PM||||I'm selling a car.  A younger female called about it.  The car is listed as "needing some work".  I've been straight with her about what it needs and what it doesn't, but I get the impression she wants a vehicle that is without flaws.  From talking to her, I don't think she knows too much about cars.  I need to sell the car, but at the same time, I don't want the buyer to be expecting one thing and get another.  I'd feel bad if it didn't work out for her.  Any opinions on this?  Thanks...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||07-29-2001||09:06 PM||||Just be straight with her... Tell her what you think the car will need and how much you think it will cost. She deserves that you and you will sleep better at night knowing you did the right thing.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||hugh||07-29-2001||09:13 PM||||Honesty is always the best policy,It might cost you a sale but you will feel better about yourself.Also if you aren't honest,then you have to remember what you said. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||07-30-2001||11:06 AM||||the last car I sold, I '72 VW bug, had some life left in it but it needed work.<P> I had it in the swap sheet for over three months. <P> some guy wanted to buy it for his daughter to commute to college with. I told him no.<P> some guy was moving to minnesoda, wanted a car that was good in the snow - mine had no heat - I told him no.<P>  some guy came over with his 16 year old son, they were looking for a project car - so I told him everything that was wrong with it, they told me no.<P>  it was like this for the whole three months. Finally a cop from the next county over came to see it - he had restored a few really old cars, model Ts and stuff, and wanted to take a wack at a beetle. I sold it to him for half the price I had it listed for.<P>   If you have a car that you wouldnt sell to a friend, make sure the buyer understands what they are getting into. If you dont feel good about selling it, donate it to a charaity - they usually take em to a scrap yard and the charity gets $50 to $100, and you get to write off the blue book value of the car on your itemized deductions (if you dont itemize then you get nothing!)<P>  whenever Im faced with this situation I always go back to the basic fact, if I wanted to get someone elses money in a less than honest manner, all I need is a gun and a ski mask.||||reg||1
Z||000004||frontal lobe||07-30-2001||04:31 PM||||Don't sell it to her, even though you have disclosed the flaws.  You can live without the money, but hurting your conscience is TOO expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||07-31-2001||01:12 AM||||" I get the impression she wants a vehicle that is without flaws...I don't want the buyer to be expecting one thing and get another. I'd feel bad if it didn't work out for her."<P>Sounds like you already know what to do.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||07-31-2001||01:29 AM||||There's nothing wrong with saying "This car's not right for you". At worst she'll be pissed and think you're a sexist bastard, but you can stand on the fact that you were right.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Urchin||07-31-2001||05:41 PM||||Ya, I think the common opinion here is the right one.  Thanks for the thoughts...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||08-01-2001||12:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>There's nothing wrong with saying "This car's not right for you". At worst she'll be pissed and think you're a sexist bastard, but you can stand on the fact that you were right.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Jeesh. If she gets crappy about it, then sell it to her. Just remind her that she was warned.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||JSocha||08-01-2001||12:55 PM||||If you do sell it to her, make sure that she signs a waiver of understanding and list all the problems you knew of and that you shared with her and keep a copy.<P>This way she can not come back and say you didn't tell me this or that.<P>Sold my '86 Dodge Ramcharger LE150 back in '97.  Told the kid what was all wrong with it and what had been fixed (including the heads).<P>Tried to get his money back telling me I didn't tell him that the heads were bad when they were infact fixed.  Good thing I had the receipt.  Found out he took it and ran it through ice cold water (as it was approacing winter time) and somehow cracked the head and the block (don't know how).<P>Judge threw it out when the kid took me to court.||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||08-01-2001||12:59 PM||||get a salesman and give him 5% to sell it for you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
